## #020 — 2026-05-25 — H1' (distribution-shape) supported with overwhelming significance: KS D=0.72, p≈10⁻⁴⁴
**Trigger:** Pre-specified K-S test on the per-feature ICC distributions per paradigm (logged in decisions.log #019 *before* running, to maintain protocol-fidelity for the H1' reframe).

**Test results (two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov, two-sided):**

| Comparison | KS D | p-value | n_a / n_b |
|------------|------|---------|-----------|
| BiomedCLIP vs radiomics-2d | **0.722** | **3.21 × 10⁻⁴⁴** | 512 / 102 |
| BiomedCLIP vs radiomics-3d | **0.679** | **4.26 × 10⁻⁴⁰** | 512 / 107 |
| radiomics-2d vs radiomics-3d | 0.246 | 2.77 × 10⁻³ | 102 / 107 |

**Interpretation:**
- BiomedCLIP-vs-radiomics: D ≈ 0.7 means the two empirical CDFs are separated by ~70 % at the worst point. Effect size is enormous and unambiguous; the paradigms behave like two genuinely different populations of features rather than noisy versions of the same thing.
- Radiomics-2D vs Radiomics-3D: statistically distinguishable (p < 0.01) with modest effect. The 2D arm sits slightly closer to the "excellent" tail than the 3D arm — mechanistically plausible because 2D shape descriptors (`shape2D_Perimeter`, `shape2D_Sphericity`) compute over fewer voxels and are less sensitive to boundary-erosion edge effects than their 3D analogues (`shape_Sphericity`, `shape_SurfaceVolumeRatio`). Worth a sentence in supplementary Discussion.

**Locked Results §3.3 paragraph (draft):** "We tested whether the per-feature ICC distributions differed between paradigms using the two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. BiomedCLIP and IBSI-aligned PyRadiomics features exhibited highly significantly different ICC distributions in both the 2D (D = 0.72, p < 10⁻⁴³) and 3D (D = 0.68, p < 10⁻³⁹) comparisons, indicating qualitatively distinct stability profiles between the two paradigms. The two PyRadiomics arms differed modestly from each other (D = 0.25, p = 0.003), with the 2D arm exhibiting marginally higher representation in the excellent stability stratum (ICC ≥ 0.90) than the 3D arm."

**Combined H1 + H1' messaging for the manuscript:** "Although both paradigms achieved comparable overall proportions of stable (ICC ≥ 0.75) features (BiomedCLIP 90.4 %, PyRadiomics-2D 91.2 %, PyRadiomics-3D 94.4 %; pairwise z-tests p > 0.19), the underlying distribution shapes differed dramatically (Kolmogorov-Smirnov D = 0.68-0.72, p < 10⁻³⁹). PyRadiomics features exhibited a bimodal pattern with 63-68 % in the excellent stratum (ICC ≥ 0.90) and a small unstable tail, while BiomedCLIP embeddings concentrated almost entirely (90 %) in the moderate-good stratum (0.75 ≤ ICC < 0.90) with zero poor (< 0.50) dimensions. This pattern is consistent with the SimCLR-style contrastive pretraining objective, which enforces approximately uniform invariance to image augmentations across all embedding dimensions, whereas IBSI radiomics has no such constraint and produces a mixture of mathematically robust features and inherently noise-sensitive ones."

**Files updated:** decisions.log only. Manuscript text update deferred until script 07 results so the H2/H3 results can be woven into the same Results §3 draft.

