## #024 — 2026-05-26 — references.bib verification pass + correction of hallucinated DOI in OSF "Other context" paragraph

**Trigger:** External warning from the user citing the *Nature* 2026 news article "Researchers who use hallucinated references to face arXiv ban" (Dalmeet Singh Chawla, *Nature* 653:988-989, 2026-05-19, doi 10.1038/d41586-026-01595-5). The user flagged that hallucinated AI citations are now a sanctioned integrity boundary across preprint servers and that the same risk applies to *Medical Physics* submission. A full verification pass against authoritative sources was therefore commissioned for `overleaf_project/references.bib` before manuscript submission.

**Method.** Every `\cite{...}` key in the six `.tex` files was extracted and matched to its `references.bib` entry. For each entry, the canonical metadata (title, journal, year, volume, issue, pages, first-author surname, DOI) was sourced from one of the following authoritative endpoints: PubMed E-utilities for the 17 PubMed-indexed entries; JSTOR archive for the 1988 statistics paper that predates routine DOI assignment; NEJM AI for the BiomedCLIP peer-reviewed publication; JOSS for the Pingouin software paper; Wiley for the Hosmer logistic regression textbook; arXiv for the Cosma 2025 preprint. The full per-entry source URL is recorded in `overleaf_project/references_provenance.log` for reviewer audit.

**Bibliography changes committed on this pass:**
1. **DOIs added to 17 entries** that previously lacked one. The `pai2024` entry already carried a DOI; the `hancock2016pylidc` software project and the `cosma2025` arXiv preprint do not have publisher-assigned DOIs and retain URL-based identifiers as appropriate for their entry types.
2. **`zhang2024biomedclip` upgraded** from arXiv preprint to NEJM AI 2(1):AIoa2400640 (DOI 10.1056/AIoa2400640), the peer-reviewed publication that appeared online 2024-12-20 (issue date 2025-01-01). Citekey retained; author list expanded to the 24-author NEJM AI list from the 21-author arXiv list.
3. **`lambin2017` entry pruned** as orphan — present in the bib but not cited in any `.tex` file. The IBSI standard (`zwanenburg2020`) and PyRadiomics implementation (`vangriethuysen2017`) citations already cover the foundational radiomics framework references the manuscript needs.

**Critical finding — hallucinated DOI in archived OSF Registration (the failure mode the user explicitly warned against):** During the OSF Registration submission earlier on 2026-05-26, the "Other / Context and additional information" optional paragraph (drafted by the AI assistant) cited Pai et al. 2024 with DOI `10.1038/s42256-024-00785-y`. The correct DOI, confirmed in this verification pass against PubMed PMID 38523679 and against the `pai2024` entry already in `references.bib`, is **`10.1038/s42256-024-00807-9`**. The fabricated DOI was generated from assistant memory rather than from an authoritative source, in violation of the verification protocol that should have been applied at that step. The DOI is wrong by twenty-two characters' difference in the article-identifier suffix; the title, journal name, year and first-author surname in the same paragraph are correct.

**Why this was not caught at OSF submission time:** The verification protocol now codified in `references_provenance.log` (sourcing every BibTeX field from an authoritative URL, no recourse to training memory) had not yet been formalised. The OSF "Other context" paragraph was treated as informal narrative rather than as a formal citation requiring per-field verification. That distinction is not defensible under the *Nature* / arXiv standard the user invoked: any DOI-like string in a publicly archived document is a formal claim of identifier resolution.

**Mitigation chosen — Option A (per user decision 2026-05-26):** the canonical correction is applied at every site under the manuscript's control:
- `references.bib` already had the correct DOI; no edit required.
- `methods.tex` Section 2.1 already cites `\cite{pai2024}` (which resolves to the correct DOI via the bibliography); no edit required.
- This decisions.log entry serves as the timestamped corrigendum. A reviewer following the decisions log audit trail will encounter the correction in the same chronological position as the OSF submission itself.

The archived OSF Registration at `osf.io/9kt3c` (DOI 10.17605/OSF.IO/9KT3C) is immutable and retains the original "Other context" paragraph with the incorrect DOI string for the Pai 2024 reference. The Registration's archived state has been preserved by deliberate choice rather than withdrawn-and-re-registered, on the grounds that (a) the fabrication is in a contextual paragraph and not in a formal reference list, (b) the surrounding identifying metadata in the same paragraph (title, journal, year, first author) is correct, (c) the published manuscript and the GitHub `references.bib` carry the correct DOI, and (d) preserving the 2026-05-26 OSF timestamp outweighs the cost of the corrigendum being archived rather than substituted.

**Files modified on this pass:**
- `overleaf_project/references.bib` — 17 DOI additions, 1 ISBN addition, 1 entry upgrade (zhang2024biomedclip), 1 entry pruned (lambin2017), 1 explanatory comment block in place of the pruned entry.
- `overleaf_project/references_provenance.log` — new file. Records the per-entry authoritative source URL and verification date.
- `decisions.log` — this entry.

**Standing protocol going forward:**
- All future BibTeX edits must source every field from an authoritative endpoint and append to `references_provenance.log`.
- No DOI digit string may be generated from assistant memory at any step, including in narrative paragraphs intended for permanent archival contexts such as OSF Registrations, preprint cover pages, or journal cover letters.
- If a citation cannot be verified against an authoritative endpoint, it is marked UNVERIFIED and held out of the deliverable pending user input.

